

<% content_for :title, "The Big Picture & Bipolar | Bipolar Sober" %>

<style>
  .big-picture-page .big-picture-copy,
  .big-picture-page .big-picture-stack > div {
    min-width: 0;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
</style>

<main class="big-picture-page">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="big-picture-hero">
    <div class="big-picture-hero-content">

      <p class="big-picture-eyebrow">
        BIPOLAR SOBER / BIPOLAR / PERSPECTIVES / THE BIG PICTURE
      </p>

      <h1>
        <span>THE</span>
        <span>BIG</span>
        <span>PICTURE.</span>
      </h1>

      <p class="big-picture-intro">
        DIFFERENT LENSES.<br>
        SAME COMPLICATED FUCKING HUMAN.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 01 -->
  <section class="big-picture-section big-picture-pink" aria-labelledby="big-picture-section-1-title">

    <div class="big-picture-section-heading">
      <p class="big-picture-number">
        01 / SO WHAT'S THE POINT?
      </p>

      <h2 id="big-picture-section-1-title">
        NOBODY<br>
        GETS THE<br>
        WHOLE THING.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="big-picture-copy">

      <p>
        When you live with bipolar disorder, you get plenty of chances to question what
        the fuck your brain is doing. Buddhism, Hinduism, Stoicism, CBT, DBT, and IFS
        offer different ways to understand your experience. Their teachings differ, but
        this overview brings together themes of awareness, acceptance, compassion,
        responsibility, and growth.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 02 -->
  <section class="big-picture-section big-picture-yellow" aria-labelledby="big-picture-section-2-title">

    <div class="big-picture-section-heading">
      <p class="big-picture-number">
        02 / BUDDHISM
      </p>

      <h2 id="big-picture-section-2-title">
        THIS WILL<br>
        CHANGE.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="big-picture-copy">

      <p>
        Buddhism begins by acknowledging dukkha: suffering and dissatisfaction. Bipolar
        disorder brings pain you did not choose. The image of two arrows distinguishes
        that pain from the extra suffering shame, self-hatred, or resistance can add.
        You can recognize what hurts and respond with care.
      </p>

      <p>
        Impermanence reminds you that thoughts, emotions, and circumstances change. A
        lifelong diagnosis does not mean every day will feel the same. Nonself invites
        you to loosen fixed labels such as “I’m a failure,” instead of making one
        episode your whole identity.
      </p>

      <p>
        Mindfulness helps you notice anger, hopelessness, or an urge before reacting
        when possible. The Eightfold Path connects awareness with compassion and ethical
        action. The Middle Way offers a guiding balance: accept reality, take
        responsibility, and keep seeking relief without demanding perfection.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 03 -->
  <section class="big-picture-section big-picture-purple" aria-labelledby="big-picture-section-3-title">

    <div class="big-picture-section-heading">
      <p class="big-picture-number">
        03 / HINDUISM
      </p>

      <h2 id="big-picture-section-3-title">
        YOU ARE<br>
        MORE THAN<br>
        THIS STATE.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="big-picture-copy">

      <p>
        Some Hindu traditions approach identity through Atman, the enduring Self beyond
        changing mental states. This differs from Buddhist nonself while offering
        another perspective on worth beyond symptoms. The gunas provide philosophical
        language for changing qualities; equanimity and dharma encourage steadiness and
        responsible action.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 04 -->
  <section class="big-picture-section big-picture-black" aria-labelledby="big-picture-section-4-title">

    <div class="big-picture-section-heading">
      <p class="big-picture-number">
        04 / STOICISM
      </p>

      <h2 id="big-picture-section-4-title">
        WHAT'S<br>
        ACTUALLY<br>
        MINE?
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="big-picture-copy">

      <p>
        Stoicism asks what is yours to influence. You cannot undo the past or guarantee
        another episode will never happen. You can prepare, follow treatment, establish
        safeguards, and seek support. A setback can become a problem to respond to,
        rather than proof that your progress vanished.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 05 -->
  <section class="big-picture-section big-picture-pink" aria-labelledby="big-picture-section-5-title">

    <div class="big-picture-section-heading">
      <p class="big-picture-number">
        05 / CBT
      </p>

      <h2 id="big-picture-section-5-title">
        CHECK THE<br>
        FUCKING<br>
        RECEIPTS.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="big-picture-copy">

      <p>
        CBT examines the cycle between thoughts, emotions, and behavior. Notice
        automatic thoughts, identify distortions, and ask what evidence supports them.
        Feeling worthless does not establish that you are worthless. Reframing and
        manageable actions offer ways to respond more deliberately.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 06 -->
  <section class="big-picture-section big-picture-yellow" aria-labelledby="big-picture-section-6-title">

    <div class="big-picture-section-heading">
      <p class="big-picture-number">
        06 / DBT
      </p>

      <h2 id="big-picture-section-6-title">
        HOLD<br>
        BOTH.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="big-picture-copy">

      <p>
        DBT brings acceptance and change together. Your emotions can make sense and your
        behavior can still need to change. Wise Mind considers feelings alongside facts;
        distress tolerance helps you navigate painful moments without adding harm.
        Skills also support boundaries, communication, and relationships.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 07 -->
  <section class="big-picture-section big-picture-purple" aria-labelledby="big-picture-section-7-title">

    <div class="big-picture-section-heading">
      <p class="big-picture-number">
        07 / IFS
      </p>

      <h2 id="big-picture-section-7-title">
        WHO'S<br>
        TALKING<br>
        RIGHT NOW?
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="big-picture-copy">

      <p>
        IFS explores the needs behind internal conflict. Managers seek control,
        firefighters seek immediate relief, and exiles carry vulnerability. Meet these
        patterns with curiosity while taking responsibility for what you do. Parts
        language is a framework; it does not explain away mania or psychosis.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 08 / ALL TOGETHER -->
  <section class="big-picture-section big-picture-black" aria-labelledby="big-picture-section-8-title">

    <div class="big-picture-section-heading">
      <p class="big-picture-number">
        08 / PUT THEM TOGETHER
      </p>

      <h2 id="big-picture-section-8-title">
        ONE<br>
        FUCKING<br>
        MOMENT.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="big-picture-copy">

      <p>
        When an urge for relief gets loud, these perspectives offer different ways to
        pause:
      </p>

      <div class="big-picture-stack">

        <div>
          <span>BUDDHISM</span>
          <p>Notice what is happening with compassion.</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>HINDUISM</span>
          <p>Remember your worth beyond this mental state.</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>STOICISM</span>
          <p>Work with what you can influence.</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>CBT</span>
          <p>Check your interpretation against the evidence.</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>DBT</span>
          <p>Accept the emotion and choose an effective response.</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>IFS</span>
          <p>Listen for the need behind the urge.</p>
        </div>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 09 / COMMON GROUND -->
  <section class="big-picture-section big-picture-pink" aria-labelledby="big-picture-section-9-title">

    <div class="big-picture-section-heading">
      <p class="big-picture-number">
        09 / COMMON GROUND
      </p>

      <h2 id="big-picture-section-9-title">
        NOTICE.<br>
        PAUSE.<br>
        CHOOSE.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="big-picture-copy">

      <p>
        Buddhist compassion includes you and the people affected by your actions.
        Illness may help explain harm while repair and accountability still matter. Make
        amends where possible, learn from your patterns, and establish safeguards. Shame
        does not need to become a permanent sentence.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 10 / BIPOLAR -->
  <section class="big-picture-section big-picture-yellow" aria-labelledby="big-picture-section-10-title">

    <div class="big-picture-section-heading">
      <p class="big-picture-number">
        10 / AND BIPOLAR?
      </p>

      <h2 id="big-picture-section-10-title">
        A LENS<br>
        ISN'T A<br>
        CURE.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="big-picture-copy">

      <p>
        These approaches can support understanding and coping alongside psychiatric
        care, medication, routines, and trusted support. Severe episodes can impair
        insight and choice. Needing outside help is part of caring for yourself.
        Philosophical insight or spiritual practice does not replace treatment.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- ENDING -->
  <section class="big-picture-ending">

    <div>

      <p class="big-picture-ending-small">
        MAYBE THERE ISN'T ONE PERFECT ANSWER.
      </p>

      <h2>
        BUILD YOUR<br>
        OWN FUCKING<br>
        <span>TOOLBOX.</span>
      </h2>

    </div>

    <div class="big-picture-ending-copy">

      <p>
        The Buddhist Middle Way helps hold this together: awareness, compassion, and
        steady practice. Keep what helps you live according to your values. You can
        learn from suffering without believing you deserved it, and build a meaningful
        life beyond your diagnosis.
      </p>

      <%= link_to "← BACK TO PERSPECTIVES",
          bipolar_perspectives_path,
          class: "big-picture-back-button" %>

    </div>

  </section>

</main>


